{
  "case_id": "2021-20-219",
  "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0000-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:00 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Andrew Rozynski",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Fifth Circuit categorically prohibited emotional distress damages in all instances under Spending Clause statutes prohibiting discrimination.\nBased on Franklin and Barnes, that decision is wrong. Franklin held that compensatory damages are available, and Barnes reaffirmed that holding. Emotional distress damages are the most common and often the only form of compensatory damage remedy for victims of intentional discrimination."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Andrew Rozynski",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Premier has not and cannot dispute that serious emotional disturbance is a particularly likely result of intentional discrimination. Premier asks this Court to disregard the Restatement rule, but there is no basis to do so."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If --if we don't agree with you that the emotional distress damages were traditionally available, do you have a -- a -- another argument?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The treatises -- the treatises all identify a very narrow -- the general rule is that emotional distress is not part of compensatory damages for breaches of contract, so they're a very narrow category of cases, as the Chief Justice said, including, you know, the -- the telegram cases and disposal-of-dead-body cases and marriage contract."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0001-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This Court has repeatedly recognized that federal funding recipients may be liable for compensatory damages when they engage in intentional conduct in violation of the clear terms of the nondiscrimination statutes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Respondents, therefore, ask this Court to hold that while funding recipients are on notice that they must pay compensation, they are not on notice that they must compensate for some of the core harms of discrimination: humiliation, degradation, and related emotional distress."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0001-0005",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "basic, very broad contract rule that the remedies that are available for a particular breach are determined by what was -- what was foreseeable, what naturally flowed, what was within the reasonable contemplation of the parties at the time of contracting."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0001-0007",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "As a preliminary matter, you can't even get your foot in the door for compensatory damages until you show intentional conduct in violation of the clear terms of a statute. And even after you prove that kind of conduct, you then have to prove actual injury."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0001-0009",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I -- I don't think so because I think that in many cases, that isn't attached. But even if that -- that was a requirement, I think because there -- it does have to be intentional conduct in violation of the clear terms, it would be satisfied in all of these cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0002-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Official statutes are in the nature of contracts, recipients of federal funding must have clear notice of the conditions that attach to the funding."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The general rule has long been that a plaintiff cannot recover for emotional distress in a contract action."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "As Justice Alito's question illustrated, emotional distress damages are notoriously difficult to quantify."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The judgment of the court of appeals should be affirmed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the prevailing rule that emotional distress damages are available in contract actions."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I guess I don't understand that answer, Mr. Shanmugam, and -- and -- and indulge me for a minute with a hypothetical."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think there are some jurisdictions that have not permitted emotional distress damages even in -- in those cases."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: What's the one -- what's the exception?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Heritage Reporting Corporation",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0003-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 79,
        "start": 79
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "if, in fact, emotional distress damages are to be made available here, that is a matter for Congress to address in the first instance."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "202ea2e7da5d69b2d5543bd3156607e39ff966d1bf73556554c1bd4e6a0f7e5e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-202ea2e7da5d-0003-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 80,
        "start": 80
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The court -- the trial court itself raised sua sponte, on its own accord, and said emotional distress damages are categorically unavailable under these statutes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2021-20-219",
      "confidence": 0.0,
